From a5307e7836aa2a4fdb506537f77ae1484aa4c8d7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Arbion Halili <a.halili@protonmail.com>
Date: Fri, 31 Jul 2026 15:24:30 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] fix(castore/fs): honour FUSE FORGET and bound the inode
 tracker

The castore filesystem never implemented forget/batch_forget, so the inode
tracker only ever grew: every lookup allocated tracker state that lived until
the mount was torn down. A long-lived virtio-fs mount serving a large store
leaks an inode (and its data and reverse-lookup entry) for every path the
guest ever touches.

Track the FUSE lookup count the protocol already defines. Every reply that
hands the kernel an Entry -- a lookup, and each readdirplus entry -- adds one
to the inode's count; forget/batch_forget draw it back down, and the tracker
evicts the inode once its last reference is gone, dropping its data and the
content->inode reverse mapping together. Content-addressed deduplication
means several names share one inode and one count, so eviction only happens
at a true zero; a re-put of the same content afterwards allocates a fresh
inode rather than resurrecting the old number.

Enumeration is the one path that hands out an inode without taking a
reference: a plain readdir returns a child's d_ino with no Entry, so those
children sit at a zero lookup count that no FORGET will ever draw down. To
keep an enumerate-only guest from leaking them, evicting a populated
directory sweeps its zero-count children. The sweep does not recurse: a
directory only becomes populated through a lookup that raised its count above
zero, so a zero-count inode is never a populated directory and evicting one
finds no further children. The sole residue left unreclaimed is the root
listing itself, which the permanent root inode pins; that is documented on
InodeTracker rather than swept.

Eviction makes stale inodes reachable, so the lifetime is closed off at both
ends:

  - The root inode is permanent: it never enters the tracker and forget
    ignores it.

  - A directory caches its children's inode numbers, and the kernel may
    forget a child while still holding the parent. get_directory_children
    reinstates any such evicted child under its original number before
    handing it out; the root-name resolver does the same for root entries,
    re-fetching and re-allocating when its cached inode has been evicted.

Unit tests cover the lookup/forget refcount cycle, partial forget, that
eviction frees the reverse map (a re-put allocates a fresh inode), restore,
and the directory sweep (both a mixed directory and an enumerate-only one);
filesystem-level tests cover a lookup/forget/revive cycle, partial forget
keeping an inode alive, the root surviving an unbounded forget, and a
readdir-then-forget reclaiming never-looked-up children.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
